  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warmer and more humid conditions push into the\nregion today with the highest odds for rain Thursday\n\nFair, dry weather will continue through this Wednesday morning \nacross much of Central and Southern PA, while mostly cloudy \nskies with occasional showers and thunderstorms will be found \nacross the Northwest Mountains and Erie Lakeshore Region.\n\nA plume of anomalously high moisture will stream into the state \nfrom the west today. A large jump in Precipitable Water (from \naround 1 inch this morning to 2 inches this afternoon - right \ninto Thursday night) will precede and accompany a cold front \nthat was located over Southern Lake Huron and Southern Michigan \nthis morning. \n\nThe cfront will interact with the warmer/much higher dewpoint \nair (in the 60s to low 70s) and trigger scattered showers and a\nfew t-storms today (mainly across northern PA). At this time, \nsevere storms are not expected with only a limited/marginal risk\nof locally heavy rainfall across the western Alleghenies. \n\nHREF 6-hour QPF Prob-Matched Mean values are generally between \nabout 0.10-0.20 of an inch this afternoon, and limited to mainly\nthe Western Half of PA. Highly localized 0.30-0.50 inch amounts\nare possible by dusk this evening. More widespread showers and \nisolated, embedded TSRA will follow for tonight and Thursday. 24\nhour QPF ENS Localized (110 km) PMM values average between \n0.2-0.5 of an inch with some highly localized 1-1.5 inch values \n(well below the FFG threshold for any flooding concerns ATTM. \n\nThe cold front will clear the Southern PA border Thursday night.\n\nMuch cooler air with low dewpoints will follow for Friday and \nSaturday. Thickening cloud cover will bring the next chance for \nrain later Saturday night into Sunday.",
